Good, this confirms fe2ccb8 only touched comments/imports/unrelated refactoring, not the null-check logic itself (just added the `// handle null sourceTrack` comment). The actual behavioral change (skip track when source has no track) was introduced by commit 98322172593acecf02869258c392cc4d08155fb1.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 98322172593acecf02869258c392cc4d08155fb1

## Reasoning

(1) **Investigation**: I ran `git log -p --follow` on `src/main/java/maud/Util.java` and searched for `retargetTrack`, `sourceTrack != null`, and the comment `handle null sourceTrack` across its history. This let me trace exactly when the `if (sourceTrack != null) { ... }` guard around the `retargetTrack` call (the code removed in the bug-fix commit `7a91a35`) was introduced, and how `retargetTrack` evolved over time.

(2) **Specific change**: Commit `9832217` (`98322172593acecf02869258c392cc4d08155fb1`, "Fri Jul 7 09:07:40 2017") refactored `retargetAnimation`/`retargetTrack`: it moved the `MyAnimation.findTrack(...)` call out of `retargetTrack` and into the caller, and in doing so added:
```java
sourceTrack = MyAnimation.findTrack(sourceAnimation, iSource);
if (sourceTrack != null) {
    BoneTrack track;
    track = retargetTrack(sourceAnimation, sourceTrack,
            sourceSkeleton, targetSkeleton, mapping, iTarget);
    result.addTrack(track);
}
```
This made `retargetAnimation` silently **skip adding a track entirely** for any target bone whose mapped source bone had no track in the given animation (i.e. an unanimated/bind-pose bone). Before this commit, `retargetTrack` unconditionally called `sourceTrack.getTimes()` with no null handling at all (it would NPE in this scenario rather than silently drop the bone), so this commit is where the "skip-on-null" behavior — the thing the fix commit specifically targets and replaces — was introduced.

The fix commit `7a91a35` removes exactly this guard and instead changes `retargetTrack` to accept a possibly-null `sourceTrack` and synthesize a single-keyframe bind-pose track when it's null, ensuring every mapped target bone always gets a track (so it doesn't retain a stale pose from some unrelated previous animation).

(3) **Evidence**: The diff of `7a91a35` is a near-exact structural inverse of the diff introduced in `9832217`: the `if (sourceTrack != null) {...}` block wrapping `retargetTrack`/`addTrack` that's deleted in the fix is the same block (modulo an added comment from `fe2ccb8`, which only touched imports/unrelated code, not this logic) that `9832217` added. No other commit between `9832217` and `7a91a35` touches this conditional or the null-handling logic in `retargetTrack`.

Confidence: high that `9832217` is the introducing commit for the specific "skip on null source track" defect targeted by this fix. One caveat: if one considers the complete absence of null-handling (present since the feature's original introduction in `2b5b2032`, which would NPE instead of silently skip) as "the same underlying bug," that earlier commit could also be cited — but since `7a91a35`'s diff precisely reverses the behavior added in `9832217` (skip-on-null → synthesize bind pose), I consider `9832217` the correct and primary answer.

